  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The System Map",
    "overview": "Choosing a card is reading a route map. A category is the origin, your cards are destinations, and how far along the line a card sits is what a dollar earns there. The world is a 1960s airline system map and its jellybean fleet: a saturated ultramarine ground, pale land rising from below, one thick yellow route, thin dashed routes in fleet colors, and three-letter codes in place of logos.\n\nThe system has two zones and they do different jobs. The ground carries the question, the map, and the headline numbers; land carries the answer, the lists, and the forms. Every screen is built this way, from Best Card to the add-purchase form. Density is that of a timetable: plain rows between thin rules, one right-aligned column of figures, no cards inside cards.\n\nThe world refuses its category's default: a balance hero, a card carousel, and soft white tiles. It must not look like a bank, and there is a single appearance, with no dark mode and no theme switch.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Two zones, ground and land, set by one class that overrides the color variables",
      "One fixed percent scale per wallet, so routes move and the ruler stays put",
      "Two colors with exactly one meaning each: yellow wins, orange was missed",
      "Three-letter codes as the only identity a card or category has",
      "One family, Archivo, spoken at three widths",
      "Phone-first, flat, and answer-first"
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Yellow Rule",
        "body": "Yellow means the winning route and its selected origin, and nothing else. In practice that is the winner's route, stop, rate, code, and tag, plus the selected category key. The logo qualifies because it is a drawn winning route. The map's origin dot and code stay white. Never use yellow for a highlight, a badge, a star, or a primary button.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Orange Rule",
        "body": "Orange means missed rewards, and nothing else. It is never an alert, a warning, or a call to action; those are Danger Red's job or nobody's.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Orange Legibility Rule",
        "body": "Orange never appears as bare text, because the raw value reads at 2.6:1 on land and 3.2:1 on the ground. On land, orange text is Missed Ink. On the ground, an orange amount sits in an orange tag with ink text.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Livery Rule",
        "body": "Yellow and orange are not in the fleet. Fleet colors identify a card and carry no value judgment, so no fleet color may suggest better or worse.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Words Too Rule",
        "body": "Color never carries meaning alone. Every map has a ranked text list beside it, the winner is also thicker and solid, and missed amounts are always written out.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Width Rule",
        "body": "Width carries voice: wide for headings, extra wide for codes, narrow only where space is tight. Do not add a second family.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Sentence Case Rule",
        "body": "The only uppercase in the system is a code. Labels and headers stay in sentence case, and no eyebrow sits above a heading.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Timetable Rule",
        "body": "Rates and amounts use tabular figures and sit in one right-aligned column, so they line up down the whole screen.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Answer Rises Rule",
        "body": "The question sits on the ground and the answer sits on land. A new screen's title, lead, and map go on ultramarine; its lists and forms go on a land panel.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Fixed Scale Rule",
        "body": "The map's percent axis is set once per wallet and does not change between categories. Motion means a card moved, never that the ruler did.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Shadow Rule",
        "body": "Only a dialog leaves the map. Anything in the page stays flat and uses zones and rules for hierarchy.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do set a zone with the land class, and read colors from the zone's variables (text, soft text, line, fill, action, focus) so a component works on both ground and land.",
      "Do put the question on the ground and the answer on land.",
      "Do take every card code from `cardCodes` in `lib/card-codes.ts`, so presets keep fixed codes and every code is unique in a wallet.",
      "Do give every card one fleet color through `cardColor` in `lib/card-colors.ts`, and use ink text on it.",
      "Do write every value in text beside any map, route, or chart.",
      "Do use Missed Ink for orange text on land, and an orange tag with ink text on the ground.",
      "Do keep rates and amounts tabular and right-aligned in one column.",
      "Do keep routes on one fixed scale, with one thick solid winner and thin dashed others.",
      "Do honor Reduce Motion in anything hand-animated, as the route tween does."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use yellow for anything except the winning route and its selected origin.",
      "Don't use orange for anything except missed rewards.",
      "Don't use raw Missed Orange as text in either zone.",
      "Don't add yellow or orange to the fleet.",
      "Don't build a balance hero, a card carousel, or soft white tiles.",
      "Don't make it look like a bank, and don't use issuer logos.",
      "Don't add a dark mode or a theme switch. There is one appearance.",
      "Don't add a second typeface. Use Archivo's width axis.",
      "Don't add page shadows, decorative gradients, or blur. The dialog holds the only shadow.",
      "Don't set uppercase on anything but a code, or put an eyebrow above a heading.",
      "Don't let the percent axis rescale between categories.",
      "Don't put Danger Red on the ground."
    ]
  }
}
